---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import SiteCards from "../../components/SiteCards.astro";
import { sites, household } from "../../lib/model";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const rows = [
  { k: "Plot", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.plot_m2.toLocaleString("en-AU")} m²` },
  {
    k: "Growing area",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) =>
      s.shared_growing_m2 ? `${s.growable_m2} + ${s.shared_growing_m2} m²` : `${s.growable_m2} m²`,
  },
  { k: "Roof", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.roof_m2} m²` },
  { k: "Existing storage", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.existing_storage_kl} kL` },
  {
    k: "Other water",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) =>
      s.other_water_kl_year ? `${s.other_water_kl_year} kL/yr bore, capped` : "—",
  },
  { k: "Rain", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.annual_rain_mm} mm/yr` },
  { k: "ETo", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.annual_eto_mm} mm/yr` },
  { k: "P/ETo", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.aridity_p_over_eto}` },
  {
    k: "Rain in driest 4 months",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${(s.climate.driest_4_months_share * 100).toFixed(1)} %`,
  },
  { k: "Mean temperature", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.annual_tmean_c} °C` },
  { k: "Days at or below 2 °C", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.frost_days_le2c}` },
  { k: "Days at or above 35 °C", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.hot_days_ge35c}` },
  { k: "Roof yield", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.roof_runoff_kl} kL/yr` },
  {
    k: "Net irrigation demand",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.net_irrigation_mm_year} mm/yr`,
  },
  {
    k: "Best fish rotation (thermal)",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => s.climate.fish_best_rotation,
  },
  { k: "Mushroom fruiting months", v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.mushroom_months}` },
  {
    k: "Insect thermal factor",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.bsf_productive_factor}`,
  },
  {
    k: "WHO excreta storage band",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${s.climate.excreta_storage_years} yr`,
  },
  {
    k: "Household N the garden absorbs",
    v: (s: (typeof sites)[number]) => `${Math.round(s.climate.n_absorption_ratio * 100)} %`,
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="The five sites"
  description="Every student designs for the same household on one of five real Australian climates. Nothing about the household changes. Everything about the site does."
  wide
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p>
    The household is fixed: {household.occupants}, needing{" "}
    {household.food_energy_mj_day} MJ/day of food energy, {household.protein_g_day} g/day
    of protein, {household.domestic_water_l_person_day} L/person/day of water, and excreting{" "}
    {household.excreted_n_kg_year} kg of nitrogen a year. Climate is the only variable, so
    the five presets are the course's entire experiment.
  </p>

  <h2>Why these five, and not five easier ones</h2>
  <p>
    An earlier version of this set had two near-identical cool-temperate sites and
    no tropical one, roofs too small to supply even the household's drinking
    water, and a food model that ignored where irrigation came from. Four rounds
    of revision later, this set is what survived.
  </p>
  <p>
    A constrained search over roughly 250,000 realistic combinations of plot size,
    roof area, growing area and site inventory could not make these climates
    equally hard — the best balanced set still spread by about 1.35 standard
    deviations on a combined ease index. <strong>So fairness does not come from the
    sites. It comes from the scoring</strong>, through per-site reference bands, which
    the <a href={withBase("/method/")}>method page</a> explains in full.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the set does achieve is that <strong>no site is easier than another on every
    indicator</strong>: there are no dominated pairs. Each site leads two teaching weeks,
    one where its climate helps and one where it hurts.
  </p>

  <h2>The cards</h2>
  <p>
    Read the strengths and constraints as carefully as the numbers. Two of the
    most important facts in this course are not scored by any indicator: Canberra's
    rainfall evenness and chill, and the Brisbane townhouse's space and strata
    limits. The fairness screen shows those two sites are easiest on nothing the
    model measures and hardest on nothing either — their real character lives in
    this prose and nowhere else.
  </p>

  <SiteCards />

  <h2>Parameters, side by side</h2>

  <Figure
    caption="Preset parameters and derived climate. Site inventory is a course assumption chosen to be ordinary for each dwelling type; climate is Bureau of Meteorology long-record monthly statistics; derived rows are screening-model outputs."
    basis="model estimate"
    source="Climate: BoM stations 070014, 015590, 040214, 023000, 014015."
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Parameter</th>
          {sites.map((s) => <th scope="col">{s.short}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.k}</th>
              {sites.map((s) => <td>{r.v(s)}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <p class="note">
    The fish rotation row is a <strong>thermal</strong> result. The model ranks species
    by temperature and knows nothing about jurisdiction — at Canberra it returns
    Murray cod, which an ACT household may not keep without a licence. See{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-05/")}>week 5</a> for what you may actually keep where you are.
  </p>

  <h2>Custom sites</h2>
  <p>
    You may propose your own site, with approval by the end of week 3. The
    requirements exist so that a custom site is neither an easier problem nor an
    unmarkable one:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the same parameter sheet as a preset, a named Bureau of Meteorology station, and a source for every value;</li>
    <li>
      values inside the preset envelope — growing area 90 to 500 m², roof 170 to
      350 m², bore allocation no more than 100 kL/year, existing storage no more
      than 45 kL — or a written justification for each exception;
    </li>
    <li>staff compute your site's bands with the published method before approval, using the same three reference designs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Approvals close at the end of week 3 because week 5 onwards depends on having
    bands to score against.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  table {
    min-width: 34rem;
    font-size: 0.93rem;
  }

  th[scope="row"] {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 500;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  td {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .note {
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
